course: dictionary
lesson: 11131
title: Wick: a candle, a lamp, and a flame
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A wick is the string in the middle of a candle. When you light it, the wick soaks up melted wax and feeds the flame. Read the words around it: «Blowing out a candle pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick.» The flame sits on the wick, so the wick must be the part that burns.
explain: Wick can also be a verb. Some sports clothes wick sweat away from your skin, so you stay cool and dry. The clues are sports clothes, sweat and away. So to wick means to draw water or sweat off.
explain: Watch out for slang. In Britain, «to get on someone's wick» means to annoy someone, and it has nothing to do with candles. Context tells you which meaning you need.
fact: A wick is the string in the middle of a candle, or the strip in an oil lamp.
fact: A wick soaks up melted wax or oil so the flame keeps burning.
fact: A long wick makes a big flame, so a candle must never be left unattended.
fact: To wick away means to draw water or sweat away from the body, as some sports clothes do.
fact: In science, liquid wax climbs up the wick to the flame. This is called capillary action.
exercise: «Blowing out a candle pushes the flame's hot gas away from the wick.» What is the wick? | The match used to light it | *The string in the middle of a candle | The melted wax on the table | The hot gas from the flame
exercise: Some sports clothes wick sweat away from your skin. What does wick mean here? | To make it wet | *To draw sweat away | To hide it under a jumper | To set it on fire
exercise: Dad says, «You're getting on my wick!» What does he mean? | He wants a candle lit | He is pleased with you | *He is annoyed with you | He is thirsty and wants a drink
test: Which part of a candle soaks up melted wax so the flame keeps burning? | Glass | *Wick | Flame | Smoke
test: In science, liquid wax climbs up the wick to the flame. What is this called? | Photosynthesis | Friction | Echo | *Capillary action
exam: A long wick makes a big flame, so why must a candle never be left unattended? | Because the wick makes a loud sound | *Because a big flame can be dangerous | Because candles must be kept cold to work | Because wax goes hard
